  "slug": "dna-circulating-9b8c",
  "summary": "Recent effective population size, ancestral recombination graphs, and mtDNA trees are now targeted by neural and hybrid inference models.",
  "synthesis": "The three preprints share a common thread: recasting population-genetic and phylogenetic inference as a learning or optimization problem over genealogical or distance-based representations. LinkedNN uses a neural network to map linkage disequilibrium decay to recent effective population size, and is reported to outperform prior methods on sparse data. ARGformer applies a transformer to ancestral recombination graphs, learning self-supervised embeddings used for population structure and ancestry retrieval. Hyb-Adam-UM addresses mtDNA phylogeny reconstruction by completing missing pairwise distance matrices with an alignment-limited Needleman-Wunsch backbone and a robust treatment of ultrametric violations. What is new is that the modeled objects are demographic and genealogical quantities themselves: effective population size, recombination graph structure, and tree-consistent distances, rather than sequence features.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-02-17"
}
